      "writtenOn": "2024-09-17T21:09:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We discussed both design options when we were designing this.\n\nOne process knows how to get services (service A), and one services consume them (service B).\n\n1. With addAccessorProvider flow, you pass a single object which hooks up A and B, and all service related functionality is hidden and implemented below this layer.\n\n2. With addAccessor, we need services A and B to implement an API like \u0027IBinder getBinder(string)\u0027. If we want functionality like \u0027list all services\u0027 then we would need to implement this separately any two services A and B.\n\nSo the double indirection has two main benefits:\n- this allows the maximum amount of logic to be re-used between different service delegation implementations.\n- this allows us to change the implementation between processes A and B arbitrarily inside of libbinder, without having to externalize the communication mechanism to this API.",
      "parentUuid": "e31a0366_e64db1d7",
      "revId": "05491648de2e0e87e060d0a932aad73a6d4e9745",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "14512958_ba096ac5",
